  "limitations": [
    "This is a source-only review, not an executed equivalence test, model qualification, or bitwise/identical-token guarantee across GPUs, process lifetimes, scheduling, or allocator histories.",
    "finish_evaluation_guard explicitly covers Torch CPU/CUDA RNG. It does not claim to snapshot Python/NumPy/global OS randomness; this review found no intentional non-Torch policy sampler in the audited owner path, but does not prove all SDK/dependency internals lack other randomness.",
    "used_window_ids, current window IDs after closure, cache/head diagnostic counters, logs, UUIDs/timestamps, process IDs and storage paths are intentionally not rolled back. Old four-slot workers accumulate those bookkeeping values; new one-slot workers start from a fresh restored owner. The audited generation path uses the current identical slot window and recipe, rather than prior-slot counter values.",
    "The original implementation already generated fresh world/episode storage identities and initial diagnostic receipts per slot. This review verifies independent construction and preservation of logical task material, not byte identity of all host metadata or rendered prompts in future executions.",
    "Each episode still creates its own soft/hard deadline. More concurrent one-slot workers can change GPU contention, wall-clock cutoff exposure, and how many independently launched slots are already in flight when a global integrity pause occurs. This is a real scheduling/resource-policy difference and must be reported; it does not add/reseed scientific slots.",
    "Hard deadline expiration may prevent the final in-process evaluation guard from completing; the existing runtime retains that interruption/unknown behavior. No successful guard is assumed for a killed or unfinished slot."
  ],
